 "result": "{\"answer\": \"Strictly speaking, none \u2014 because on 1 January 2006 the IAU had no formal definition of a \\\"planet\\\" and had never adopted a resolution enumerating the planets. By long-standing convention, however, the IAU (like the rest of the astronomical community) treated the Solar System as having nine planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune and Pluto. The matter was unsettled at that moment: the object 2003 UB313 (later named Eris), announced in July 2005 and larger than Pluto, had prompted claims of a \\\"tenth planet,\\\" and an IAU working group was deliberating on a definition. That definition came only with IAU Resolution B5 at the General Assembly in Prague on 24 August 2006, which set the count at eight planets and reclassified Pluto as a \\\"dwarf planet.\\\"\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"abstained\": false}",
